<DocsLayout title="Domains, routing & webhooks" description="Understand project hostnames, Caddy routing, Cloudflare ingress, and GitHub push delivery.">
<section><p class="overline">Routing model</p><h2>One public domain, project subdomains</h2><p>Each project receives a hostname below the configured MyPaaS domain. Cloudflare Tunnel forwards the root and wildcard hostnames to Caddy; Caddy then routes dashboard/API traffic, static releases, and explicitly routed runtimes.</p><ol class="steps"><li>Cloudflare Tunnel receives public HTTPS traffic.</li><li>Caddy is the MyPaaS front door.</li><li>Static projects are served from their active release directory.</li><li>Container-backed projects are resolved onto the managed routing network.</li></ol></section>
<section><h2>Project route settings</h2><DocMedia src={settingsShot} alt="MyPaaS project settings and route configuration" title="Project settings" source="MyPaaS dashboard" note="captured 13 Aug 2026" /><p>Runtime route activation is fail-closed: MyPaaS validates the intended runtime before attaching the managed routing alias and activating the Caddy upstream.</p></section>
<section><h2>GitHub push webhooks</h2><p>Git-backed projects expose a webhook URL and per-project signing value in project settings. Add the webhook to the repository, select push events, and target the branch configured on the project.</p><DocMedia title="GitHub repository webhook" source="GitHub" note="vendor UI · capture pending" placeholder="Capture Repository Settings → Webhooks → Add webhook with the Payload URL and push-event selection visible. Hide every real signing value." /><p>Only valid signed pushes to the configured branch are accepted. Registry-image projects do not use Git webhooks because they do not have a Git source.</p></section>
</DocsLayout>
